#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138297 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138385 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a962db4f-c6bf-5631-95b2-c7f42b1236d6" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Biquadratic Expressions as Perfect Squares"}] :question/id #uuid "15bbccd7-d0ce-50bb-80bb-777b5d0a7245" :question/math-academy-id "e-4360" :question/problem "Factor $x^4-4x^2+4.$" :question/worked-solution "We can introduce a new variable $z=x^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+4 & = \\\\ {({x}^{2})}^{2}-4({x}^{2})+4 & = \\\\ {z}^{2}-4z+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic as the square of a difference, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-4z+4 & = \\\\ (z-2)^{2} & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138304 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138411 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee76fa0d-93f1-5b0b-b9e4-4c081741078e" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Biquadratic Expressions as Quadratic Trinomials"}] :question/id #uuid "12f9f52c-0baa-557b-83b7-d80c8a34c409" :question/math-academy-id "e-5018" :question/problem "Factor $t^4 - t^2 - 2.$" :question/worked-solution "We can introduce a new variable $z = t^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-2 & = \\\\ {({t}^{2})}^{2}-{t}^{2}-2 & = \\\\ {z}^{2}-z-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-z-2 & = \\\\ (z+1)(z-2) & = \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138305 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138412 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e836ca22-abd9-5812-bbf7-3b057e3d6c4b" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Biquadratic Expressions as Quadratic Trinomials With Leading Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "f503abb5-10a5-56bf-b07b-7ebe1ed04049" :question/math-academy-id "e-5019" :question/problem "Factor $2p^4 + 5p^2 + 3.$" :question/worked-solution "We can introduce a new variable $t = p^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{p}^{4}+5{p}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ 2{({p}^{2})}^{2}+5{p}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+5t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+5t+3 & = \\\\ (t+1)(2t+3) & = \\\\ ({p}^{2}+1)(2{p}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138385 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a962db4f-c6bf-5631-95b2-c7f42b1236d6" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Biquadratic Expressions as Perfect Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4396c708-a95e-5406-a956-0aecbef4f251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{({h}^{2}-5)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529737} {:answer/id #uuid "68b56254-a46c-57aa-931f-ced0cf55c182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(h+5)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529844} {:answer/id #uuid "ce748f9b-bd03-5e52-a315-b3139f1d9ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(h-5)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530098} {:answer/id #uuid "edc93743-8de4-57bd-95d7-e621f4ff912e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{({h}^{2}-10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530176} {:answer/id #uuid "f005ef11-2c85-5e11-a5c8-08759fad9a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{({h}^{2}-5h)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4396c708-a95e-5406-a956-0aecbef4f251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{({h}^{2}-5)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529737} :answer-field/id #uuid "edff1060-10e1-5ab4-841b-25b2bf6e7869" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a747aa94-8aba-509b-9a98-0e8364a093c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-112339" :question/problem "Factor ${h}^{4}-10{h}^{2}+25.$" :question/worked-solution "We can introduce a new variable $z={h}^{2}$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{4}-10{h}^{2}+25 & ={({h}^{2})}^{2}-10({h}^{2})+25 \\\\  & ={z}^{2}-10z+25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic as the square of a sum, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $h:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-10z+25 & ={(z-5)}^{2} \\\\  & ={({h}^{2}-5)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138385 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a962db4f-c6bf-5631-95b2-c7f42b1236d6" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Biquadratic Expressions as Perfect Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21b4eb38-5df9-518f-b667-e5cd965edc07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(9{n}^{2}+6n)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529651} {:answer/id #uuid "64854096-937f-5bd4-ba50-4fbad5511099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(9{n}^{3}+1)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529827} {:answer/id #uuid "6de00090-452f-5c68-a401-1ce8368de21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(3n+1)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529862} {:answer/id #uuid "a5692942-1c9e-5f6c-bb84-c68b63ab5f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175